# Resource bounds and fail-closed behavior

A project can be structurally valid and still ask for unreasonable work. A long
acyclic chain can be deeply nested. A small fragment can be referenced often enough
to produce a large expansion. A language server can announce a message larger than
the editor should allocate, and a compiler can write without end even when it never
finishes a useful document.

Literate treats resource limits as policy boundaries. When a required operation
crosses a boundary, the safe result is a diagnostic and no new product—not a
truncated program, a half-parsed protocol message, or a partially replaced book.
Optional presentation can become unavailable while its underlying document remains
readable, but it must say so explicitly.

## Valid does not mean affordable

Graph validation answers whether references exist and whether expansion contains a
cycle. It does not by itself bound the amount of text copied. If `literal(F)` is
the text written directly in fragment `F`, an approximate byte model is:

```text
bytes(expand(F)) ≈ bytes(literal(F)) + Σ bytes(expand(Rⱼ))
```

The sum has one term for every reference occurrence `Rⱼ` in `F`, and `bytes` means
the UTF-8 size of the resulting text. The equation is approximate because preserved
indentation, line-ending normalization, and later templates can add or alter bytes.
Repeated references still appear repeatedly in the sum. Even when each family is
parsed once and cached, inserting a cached expansion contributes its bytes at every
use. Acyclic graphs can therefore create outputs much larger than their source.

Depth is a related but different resource. Literate drives fragment expansion with
an explicit frame stack rather than the JavaScript call stack. That prevents a
deep valid graph from becoming accidental language-level recursion. It is not a
promise that arbitrary projects fit in memory or finish quickly. Structural
handling and resource quotas solve different problems.

This distinction is important because limits must describe the actual protected
boundary. A message-size cap does not bound the whole session. A subprocess timeout
does not bound memory used by every descendant. A browser panel budget does not
bound the size of the source project. Honest bounds are local and named.

## Think in a vector of budgets

One number cannot describe all costly work. Let the operation's observed use be a
vector

```text
U = (u_depth, u_bytes, u_segments, u_messages, u_time, u_processes)
B = (b_depth, b_bytes, b_segments, b_messages, b_time, b_processes)
```

`U` records observed use and `B` the corresponding accepted budgets. The operation
is accepted only when every enforced component fits:

```text
accept exactly when u[i] <= b[i] for every enforced dimension i
```

The formula is simply a checklist. It reminds us that staying below a byte limit
does not excuse exceeding a time limit, and that limiting the number of objects
does not bound their combined encoded size.

```mermaid
flowchart TD
    Input[Captured input] --> Count[Measure relevant resources]
    Count --> Fits{Every enforced budget fits?}
    Fits -->|No| Reject[Diagnostic or unavailable capability]
    Fits -->|Yes| Work[Perform bounded work]
    Work --> Complete{Complete valid result?}
    Complete -->|No| Reject
    Complete -->|Yes| Publish[Expose the whole result]
```

The arrows show a gate, not a claim that every cost can be known in advance. Some
budgets are checked incrementally while work proceeds. What matters is that
crossing one cannot be reinterpreted as successful completion.

## Bound data before allocating from it

A length supplied by another process is a request, not permission. The language
client reads and validates the protocol header before allocating the announced
body. It limits header bytes, body bytes, queued output, pending requests,
listeners, synchronized documents, and retained diagnostics. Invalid framing or an
oversized message fails the session rather than attempting to resynchronize inside
untrusted bytes.

The publication helper applies the same idea to its one-request input. It counts
bytes while reading the stream, enforces a deadline, and only then parses the
complete JSON envelope. Image and diagram planners check fixed item and aggregate
budgets at their planning boundaries. Font and license companions are validated for
paths, manifest shape, presence, and file kind, but currently have no dedicated byte
or count budgets.

Three details make such limits meaningful:

- count encoded bytes when the transport is byte-based, rather than JavaScript
  characters or human-visible glyphs;
- reject impossible or unsafe declared lengths before allocation;
- include framing, escaping, or retained metadata when those consume memory too.

A string of non-ASCII text illustrates the first point. Its character count may be
smaller than its UTF-8 byte count. A transport budget measured in characters could
therefore admit more memory than its name suggests.

## Bound collections as well as items

Small items can become expensive in large numbers. Literate places limits at
collection boundaries such as pending protocol requests, synchronized documents,
diagnostic reports, publication assets, fragment-exploration parts, and rendered
segments. These limits protect bookkeeping and traversal even when each individual
entry is modest.

Collection policy depends on the feature's role. A required publication input that
exceeds its accepted set causes publication to fail. A bounded diagnostic cache may
discard old or oversized cached reports because the cache is an aid, not the source
of truth. The distinction is not “strict versus lenient”; it is whether omission
could make an incomplete product look complete.

This gives a useful rule:

> Reject incomplete required meaning. Degrade only optional presentation, and make
> the degradation visible.

## Browser work needs a lifetime budget

Optional fragment exploration can recursively reveal referenced contributions. A
click may therefore create further controls, panels, text nodes, and links. Limiting
only the initial payload would leave later DOM growth unbounded.

Literate gives one initialized document budgets for payload bytes, nesting layers,
open panels, visited parts, rendered segments, and retained panel text. A requested
panel is costed before its candidate DOM is attached. Duplicate part identities
are rejected rather than silently rendered twice. Closing a panel releases the
cost owned by that panel and its descendants.

```mermaid
stateDiagram-v2
    [*] --> Collapsed
    Collapsed --> Measuring: request expansion
    Measuring --> Expanded: complete panel fits
    Measuring --> Refused: any panel budget exceeded
    Expanded --> Collapsed: close and release ownership
    Refused --> Collapsed: keep baseline link and source readable
```

This state diagram concerns the optional explorer, not ordinary document links.
Refusal does not erase the chapter or truncate code. The baseline publication
remains readable, and the control can explain that exploration is unavailable.
That is fail-closed behavior at the capability boundary rather than failure of the
whole book.

## Subprocesses need more than a timeout

Publication and language intelligence depend on child processes. A timeout is
necessary, but it is not sufficient. A process can fill stdout or stderr before
the deadline, keep inherited pipes open after its main child exits, or leave
descendants running.

Literate's process boundaries combine several controls:

1. spawn a named executable with an argument vector rather than a shell command;
2. bound captured output across the streams that are retained;
3. apply a deadline to the complete invocation, including pipe closure;
4. terminate only the lifetime the adapter actually owns;
5. accept only the expected output type and size after successful exit;
6. clean temporary resources while preserving actionable diagnostics.

Ownership differs by adapter. Mermaid rendering and PDF compilation use POSIX
process groups so browser or compiler descendants can be terminated together.
Pandoc conversion, language-server sessions, and self-host verification own their
direct child, not an arbitrary descendant tree. The editor publication helper is
also a direct child, but cancellation is deliberately graceful: the editor waits
for transaction cleanup rather than imposing an automatic hard-kill fallback.

PDF compilation illustrates the last two points. A zero exit status is not enough:
the expected PDF must be a bounded, nonempty regular file with an appropriate
header, and it must remain the same file while being read. This is still not a full
PDF proof. It is a deliberately narrow acceptance check before publication.

External tools run only on trusted inputs. Time, output, and filesystem boundaries
limit accidents and denial of service; they do not turn TeX, Lua, a browser, or a
language server into a security sandbox.

## Failure must preserve the last coherent state

Limits are most valuable when they are checked before irreversible effects. The
engine plans every output in memory and returns an empty file list on generation
diagnostics. Publication conversion finishes before its output transaction begins.
The filesystem adapter preflights the complete destination set before creating
output directories or replacing files.

```mermaid
flowchart TD
    Snapshot[Accepted snapshot] --> Validate[Validate graph and policy]
    Validate --> Build[Build complete candidate result]
    Build --> Gate{Diagnostic or enforced limit exceeded?}
    Gate -->|Yes| Old[Keep previous outputs]
    Gate -->|No| Preflight[Preflight every destination]
    Preflight --> Commit[Stage and commit as one batch]
```

The diagram omits rollback detail. Its point is temporal: expensive or fallible
semantic work belongs before commitment. If a protocol reply, diagram render, or
compiler output is incomplete, no destination should be opened merely because an
earlier stage succeeded.

Truncation is particularly dangerous for generated source. Suppose a segment
budget is `S` and expansion requires `S + 1` segments. Returning the first `S`
segments would produce a different program while presenting it as successful.
The only honest outcomes are a complete accepted expansion or rejection.

## Fail closed at the right scope

“Fail closed” does not require shutting down every feature after every local
problem. It requires refusing the authority that could make the uncertain result
harmful.

- An invalid generation graph returns no generated files.
- An oversized protocol frame closes the affected session and rejects pending work.
- A stale language reply produces no mapped result or edit.
- An over-budget exploration panel is not attached to the document.
- A failed PDF build leaves the previous published destination untouched.
- A rollback failure reports recovery locations instead of claiming restoration.

The scope follows the capability. Read-only inspection may retain partial metadata
that helps locate a malformed fragment, but partial metadata is not permission to
write outputs. A preview may show an unavailable explorer, but it must not invent
missing fragment content.

## Limits are policy, not proof of safety

Every bound has a tradeoff. A low threshold rejects legitimate large work; a high
threshold may not protect an interactive host soon enough. Limits should be based
on the protected environment, tested at and just beyond their boundaries, and
reported with enough context for an author to reduce the request.

They also need review when representations change. Adding escaping can increase
serialized bytes. Retaining a new diagnostic field can enlarge a cache. Rendering
a new node type can increase DOM ownership. A bound copied mechanically from an
older representation may no longer bound what its name claims.

Most importantly, local bounds do not add up to a universal resource guarantee.
The core engine still consumes memory proportional to accepted source, graph, and
expanded output; external processes have resources beyond the streams Literate
counts; filesystems can stall; and providers can violate assumptions. The design
reduces these risks through explicit boundaries and conservative rejection, but it
does not promise operation under every load.

The next chapter turns from runtime policy to contributor practice. Keeping these
guarantees trustworthy requires a development method in which the explanation,
generated program, tests, and distributable artifacts are reviewed together.
